proton_raw:
Solar Activity: 
Low solar activity is expected to continue with a chance of isolated Moderate-class flares.



Solar Wind / Geomagnetic Activity: &nbsp;
In the absence of any geoeffective coronal holes or Earth directed CMEs solar wind speeds are expected to remain at background. Geomagnetic activity will be Quiet to Unsettled with a chance of Active intervals on Day 1 (4 Aug).&nbsp;


Energetic Particles / Solar Radiation:&nbsp;GOES18 &gt;10MeV proton flux is expected to continue at Background with no further enhancement currently forecast..

Solar Activity: 
Low solar activity is expected to continue with a slight chance of isolated Moderate-class flares.



Solar Wind / Geomagnetic Activity: 
In the absence of any geoeffective coronal holes or Earth directed Coronal Mass Ejections, solar wind speeds are expected to reduce to Background on Day 1 (05 Aug), though Enlil suggests they could increase to Slightly Elevated on Day 4 (08 Aug) due to a possible glancing blow from a Coronal Mass Ejections . Geomagnetic activity is likely to be Quiet to Unsettled (Kp0-3), although there is a small chance it could increase on Day 4 (08 Aug) due to a possible glancing blow from a Coronal Mass Ejection or connection to Coronal Hole. This is very low confidence.&nbsp;



Energetic Particles / Solar Radiation: 
GOES18 &gt;10MeV proton flux is expected to continue at Background.&nbsp;

Solar Activity:&nbsp;
Low or Very Low activity is expected to continue, with further Common class flares likely to continue until the region in the far northwest rotates across the western limb.



Solar Wind / Geomagnetic Activity:&nbsp; 
Slightly elevated solar winds probably declining back to Low levels. A further CME arrival is then possible with a glancing impact on day 2-3 (11-12 Aug). This is due to the 08 Aug north-centre disc filament eruption, although any observed arrival is low confidence, with the CME mainly passing north of Earth. However this could arrive alongside the onset of weak fast winds. Geomagnetic activity is expected to be mainly Quiet. However any CME glance or fast wind onset later on day 2 or day 3 (11-12 Apr) will bring a further chance for a spell of Unsettled to Active geomagnetic activity with a probability of isolated Minor Geomagnetic Storm intervals.



Energetic Particles / Solar Radiation: 
The count rate of energetic particles (high energy protons) is expected to persist at Background.

Solar Activity: 
Activity is expected to remain Low, with only a slight chance of an isolated M-class flare.&nbsp;



Solar Wind / Geomagnetic Activity:
 CH86/+ is expected to connect with Earth from later Day 1 (13 Aug), or more likely through Day 2 (14 Aug), but this is also low confidence.&nbsp;


Geomagnetic activity may increase late Day 1 (13 Aug) or more likely through Day 2 (14 Aug) upon connection to CH86/+, to bring a likelihood of Active intervals and a chance of G1/Minor geomagnetic storms, which may also continue into Day 3 (15 Aug), before gradually waning on Day 4 (16 Aug).&nbsp; &nbsp;



Energetic Particles / Solar Radiation: 
GOES18 &gt;10MeV proton flux is expected to continue at Background.

Solar Activity: 
Activity will most likely remain Low, however there is a chance of isolated Moderate-class flares, most likely from AR4507
.&nbsp;




Solar Wind / Geomagnetic Activity: 
A CME (coronal mass ejection) associated with a filament eruption on 14 Aug may reach Earth early Day 1 (18 Aug). A second CME associated with a common class flare from a region in the west of the disc at 16/1928 UTC may deliver a glancing blow at Earth on Day 3 (20 Aug). Confidence for both these CMEs is very low.&nbsp;


Coronal hole fast winds may arrive during this period, perhaps late on Day 1 (18 Aug), but more likely through Day 2 (19 Aug), before gradually declining through Days 3-4 (20-21 Aug). Solar wind speeds reached 700 km/s on last rotation, but there is significant uncertainty regarding possible speeds this time around.&nbsp;


Geomagnetic activity is expected to be Quiet to Unsettled (Kp1-3) until the possible CME arrival early Day 1 (18 Aug), at which point activity may increase to give Active/ G1 minor storm intervals and a slight chance of G2/Moderate geomagnetic storms. Activity may then wane until the onset of the HSS from CH87/- or CH88/- from later Day 1, or more likely through Day 2 (19 Aug), which along with glancing CME effects on Day 3 (20 Aug) keeps a chance of G1/Minor Storms through Day 2 and 3, before activity likely returns to mostly Quiet to Unsettled levels on Day 4 (21 Aug).&nbsp;



Energetic Particles / Solar Radiation: 
GOES18 &gt;10MeV proton flux is likely to continue at background levels.

Solar Activity: 
Activity is likely to be Low, with a chance of isolated Moderate-class flares.



Solar Wind / Geomagnetic Activity:&nbsp; 
A Coronal Mass Ejection associated with the C8.2 flare from 16 August may glance Earth on Day 1 (20 Aug), although is most likely to pass to the north. Earth has now connected to the high speed stream of a coronal hole, with ongoing fast winds expected. It is also possible we may see connection to another coronal hole, perhaps leading to a prolonged period of Elevated to Strong solar winds. This combined with a possible Coronal Mass Ejection glance on Day 1 (20 Aug) will give an ongoing chance of isolated G1/Minor Storm intervals. While there is some uncertainty regarding the coronal holes, the overall fast wind conditions are expected to continue into Day 2 (21 Aug), as supported by MOSWOC Enlil, before showing signs of gradually easing from Day 3 (22 Aug). Geomagnetic activity is expected to remain Quiet to Unsettled (Kp 1-3) overall, with a chance for periods of Active to G1/Minor Storm (Kp 4-5), mainly on Day 1 (20 Aug).&nbsp;&nbsp;



Energetic Particles / Solar Radiation: 
GOES-18 &gt; MeV proton flux is at background with only a slight chance of rising given any eruptive events from the disc.&nbsp;

Solar Activity: 
Activity is likely to be Low to Moderate, with a chance of further Moderate-class flares and a slight chance of a Strong flare, mostly from the region near the northeast limb. A new or returning region is expected to rotate onto the disc, from the far side, most likely during Day 3 (23 Aug). However, this region is not expected to significantly influence solar activity.



Solar Wind / Geomagnetic Activity:&nbsp; 
A Coronal Mass Ejection (CME) associated with a Common-class flare from 16 August is now considered to have missed Earth. Earth is currently under the influence of a solar wind enhancement from one or two coronal holes in the western hemisphere. The solar wind enhancement is expected to gradually decline through the period, from initial Elevated speeds around 500 km/s down to Background levels during Days 3 and 4 (23 and 24 Aug). Geomagnetic activity is expected to remain Quiet to Unsettled overall, with a chance for periods of Active (Kp 4), mainly on Day 1 (21 Aug).&nbsp;



Energetic Particles / Solar Radiation: 
GOES-18 &gt; MeV proton flux is at background with only a slight chance of rising given any eruptive events from the disc.&nbsp;

Solar Activity: 
Solar activity is expected to remain Low or Very Low with only a slight chance of reaching Moderate levels should any isolated Moderate-class flares occur.



Solar Wind / Geomagnetic Activity:
 A low-confidence, but very slow Coronal Mass Ejection could arrive at Earth on Day 2 (14 Sep), but any effects (should an impact occur) are expected to be minor.&nbsp;


&nbsp;The next significant solar wind enhancement is due to take place between late Day 1 (13 Sep) and early Day 2 (14 Sep) with the transition into the fast winds of a coronal hole. This is a persistent feature and matches well with an equivalent position relative to 27-day recurrence, so similar wind are expected. Solar wind speeds on the last rotation reached around 400km/s on the equivalent of early Day 2, then increasing to around 550-600km/s (elevated to strong) into early Day 3 (15 Sep).


On Day 3 (15 Sep) a second recurrent fast wind enhancement is expected from another coronal hole. This feature may connect with Earth earlier than the equivalent period on the last rotation. Solar winds of around 600km/s were observed previously and it is considered likely that similarly high speeds will be reached between Days 3 and 4 (15-16 Sep).&nbsp;


Geomagnetic activity will start the period at Quiet or perhaps Unsettled levels, increasing later on Day 1 (13 Sep) with peak Active levels likely and a slight chance of Kp5/G1 intervals occurring. Into Day 2 and 3 (14 and 15 Sep), mainly Unsettled to Active geomagnetic activity is forecast, but with a reasonable chance of isolated Kp5/G1 intervals being observed. During Day 4 (16 Sep) activity should be at Quiet to Active levels, with a decreasing likelihood of higher levels of geomagnetic activity over time.



Energetic Particles / Solar Radiation:&nbsp;
GOES18 &gt;10MeV proton flux is at background with no Solar Radiation storms expected.
